JAVA LOOPS - NOTES

1. WHAT IS A LOOP?

I

A loop is used to execute a block of code repeatedly until a condition
becomes false.

Instead of:

System.out.println("Hello");
System.out.println("Hello");
System.out.println("Hello");

We can write:

for (int i= 1; i <= 3; i++) {
System.out.println("Hello");

}

2. TYPES OF LOOPS IN JAVA

Loop

for
while
do-while
enhanced for

Use

When the number of iterations is known
When repetition depends mainly on a condition
When, the code must execute at least once
Used to iterate through arrays and collections

3. FOR LOOP

Syntax:

for (initialization; condition; update) {

// statements

}

Example:

for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {

System.out.println(i);

}

Output:

1
2
3
4
5


4. PARTS OF FOR LOOP

for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++)

+

+

Update
Condition
Initialization

Initialization:

int i = 1

Runs only once when the loop starts.

Condition:

i <= 5

Checked before every iteration.

If true -> Execute loop
If false -> Stop loop

